92186271920 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186272088 #edb/ref 1069 "q-140440" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186272088 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186272088 #edb/ref 1071 "Given that $‖u‖=5$ and $u\\cdot v=-15$, find the smallest possible value of $‖v‖.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186272088 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "bd33e455-0f47-57d5-9add-73b07f36d75e" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186272088 #edb/ref 1074 "Using the Cauchy-Schwarz inequality, we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|u\\cdot v| & \\le ‖u‖\\cdot ‖v‖ \\\\ |-15| & \\le 5\\cdot ‖v‖ \\\\ 15 & \\le 5\\cdot ‖v‖.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we multiply both sides of the inequality by $\\frac{1}{5}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{5}\\cdot 15 & \\le \\frac{1}{5}\\cdot 5\\cdot ‖v‖ \\\\ 3 & \\le ‖v‖\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the smallest possible value of $‖v‖$\nis  $3.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186272089 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186271852 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186272089 #edb/ref 1069 "q-140442" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186272089 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186272089 #edb/ref 1071 "Consider the vector $u=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ \\sqrt{k} \\\\ -\\sqrt{k} \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}].$ Given that $u\\cdot v=-2$ and $‖v‖=1$, what is the smallest possible value of $k?$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186272089 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "a38c7df6-58f3-5ed8-95a4-2f7e4b402435" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186272089 #edb/ref 1074 "We will use the Cauchy-Schwarz inequality,\n\n$$\n|u\\cdot v|\\le ‖u‖\\cdot ‖v‖.\n$$\n\nWe are given all the quantities in the inequality above, except for $‖u‖.$ So first, we compute $‖u‖$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖u‖ & =\\sqrt{{1}^{2}+(\\sqrt{k})^{2}+(-\\sqrt{k})^{2}+(-1)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{2+2k}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, using the Cauchy-Schwarz inequality, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|u\\cdot v| & \\le ‖u‖\\cdot ‖v‖ \\\\ |-2| & \\le \\sqrt{2+2k}\\cdot (1) \\\\ 2 & \\le \\sqrt{2+2k} \\\\ {2}^{2} & \\le {(\\sqrt{2+2k})}^{2} \\\\ 4 & \\le 2+2k \\\\ 2 & \\le 2k \\\\ k & \\ge 1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the smallest possible value of $k$ is $1.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186272094 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186271629 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186272094 #edb/ref 1069 "q-140455" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186272094 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186272094 #edb/ref 1071 "Let $A(1,0,-1,2)$ and $B(4,8,2,-2).$ Find the cosine of the angle $∠AOB$, where $O(0,0,0,0)$ is the origin." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186272094 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "ae7334f7-ce11-500e-b881-3d819bbe315f" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186272094 #edb/ref 1074 "First, let\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a=\\overset{\\to }{OA}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}],\\,b=\\overset{\\to }{OB}=[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 8 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAlso, let $\\theta =m∠AOB$ be the angle between the vectors $a$ and $b.$ Then, we can find $\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta $ using the following formula:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & =\\frac{a\\cdot b}{‖a‖\\,‖b‖} \\\\  & =\\frac{{a}_{1}{b}_{1}+{a}_{2}{b}_{2}+{a}_{3}{b}_{3}+{a}_{4}{b}_{4}}{\\sqrt{{a}_{1}^{2}+{a}_{2}^{2}+{a}_{3}^{2}+{a}_{4}^{2}}\\,\\sqrt{{b}_{1}^{2}+{b}_{2}^{2}+{b}_{3}^{2}+{b}_{4}^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1\\cdot 4+0\\cdot 8+(-1)\\cdot 2+2\\cdot (-2)}{\\sqrt{{1}^{2}+{0}^{2}+(-1)^{2}+{2}^{2}}\\,\\sqrt{{4}^{2}+{8}^{2}+{2}^{2}+(-2)^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{-2}{\\sqrt{6}\\cdot \\sqrt{88}} \\\\  & =-\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{6}\\cdot 2\\sqrt{22}} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{132}}\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186324019 #edb/ref 1080 "\\frac{\\pi }{7}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186324019 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186324019 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "053a0d78-9998-5fa8-81c1-ec3b9c782255" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186324020 #edb/ref 1080 "\\frac{2\\pi }{7}" #edb/ref 175